              it is created; if it does exist, is a regular file and  the  nnoo‐‐
              cclloobbbbeerr  option  is  set, an error occurs, otherwise the file is
              truncated.  Note that this means the command _c_m_d  _<  _f_o_o  _>  _f_o_o
              will  open _f_o_o for reading and then truncate it when it opens it
              for writing, before _c_m_d gets a chance to actually read _f_o_o.

       >>|| _f_i_l_e
              same as >>, except the file is truncated, even if  the  nnoocclloobbbbeerr
              option is set.

       >>>> _f_i_l_e
              same  as  >>, except the file an existing file is appended to in‐
              stead of being truncated.  Also, the file is  opened  in  append
              mode, so writes always go to the end of the file (see _o_p_e_n(2)).

       << _f_i_l_e standard  input  is  redirected  from  _f_i_l_e, which is opened for
              reading.

       <<>> _f_i_l_e
              same as <<, except the file is opened for reading and writing.

       <<<< _m_a_r_k_e_r
